190
00:25:22.140 --> 00:25:39.870
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): Well, it's not… it's… yeah, I wouldn't… I don't call it infer… site inference at all. It's just a… it's like a bidirectional type check… it's bi-directional type checking, and there's a lot… I mean, there's lots of stuff that's been done about it. Dart… DART does a form of it. The down… the down… you… you can design it so that it has different types at different use… use sites.

191
00:25:40.230 --> 00:25:55.539
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): Or you could just say that… or you could just say that it's monomorphized. You have to… if you… if you say const X equals 1, that's either a UN8 literal or a field literal. It could never be both, and you have to say const Y equals 1 if you want it to be something else.

192
00:25:56.130 --> 00:26:04.879
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): For sure. There's some… but the… there is a usability issue there, which is that it's, there's lots of corner cases.

193
00:26:04.990 --> 00:26:08.860
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): And it's not… and they're not entirely predictable or understandable to programmers.

194
00:26:09.280 --> 00:26:22.350
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): And then programmers always want more. They say, well, I can see that here I intend this to be this type, like… like passing values into closures and returning them, and they say, I can clearly see that I want this to have this type, why can't you?

195
00:26:22.780 --> 00:26:25.680
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): So there's a… that's the downside.

196
00:26:27.790 --> 00:26:45.459
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): The downside of the literal syntax is that we will pretty soon have a dozen different field types, and so it just doesn't scale very well to say that we're going to invent some kind of shorthand literal syntax for every single one of them, and that a verbose literal syntax is not better than writing as field.

197
00:26:46.960 --> 00:26:51.490
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): And the third alternative is that we just actually say, you can write S-field.

198
00:26:51.880 --> 00:27:07.989
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): you have to write a field. I think that people have decided… I think that people early on, in our own examples, indicated that this was the thing that they should believe, is that performing arithmetic in the BLS scalar field was a good thing for them to do, because they didn't have to write, they didn't have to check for overflow.

199
00:27:08.140 --> 00:27:27.529
